Fill every compressed PFSC sector instead of trusting one DeflateStream.Read.

ReadSector inflated each 64 KiB sector with a single ds.Read(output, 0,
BlockSz) and ignored the count it returned. Stream.Read has always been free to
return less than asked for, and since .NET 6 DeflateStream routinely does
(dotnet/runtime "partial byte reads in streams" breaking change). The tail of
the sector then keeps whatever the previous sector left in the reused buffer.
Nothing reports it: file sizes come out right, small files often survive, and
pkg_validate still passes.

In practice: Bloodborne (CUSA03173) extracted this way has thousands of .dcx
archives that no longer inflate, and the game dies at shader setup with
"shaderBinarySize 2660 is not equal to Program size 9527387880"
(deadinside28/bloodborne_pc#81). Unfixed upstream as of 92a4e7f8e5.

A sector that ends before it is full is now an error rather than silently
short: every PFSC sector inflates to exactly BlockSz.

--- a/LibOrbisPkg/PFS/PFSCReader.cs
+++ b/LibOrbisPkg/PFS/PFSCReader.cs
@@ -84,7 +84,16 @@
         using (var bufStream = new MemoryStream(sectorBuf))
         using (var ds = new DeflateStream(bufStream, CompressionMode.Decompress))
         {
-          ds.Read(output, 0, hdr.BlockSz);
+          // A single Read may return fewer bytes than asked for (always
+          // allowed, and routine since .NET 6), so loop until the sector is full.
+          int total = 0;
+          while (total < hdr.BlockSz)
+          {
+            int n = ds.Read(output, total, hdr.BlockSz - total);
+            if (n == 0)
+              throw new InvalidDataException("PFSC sector inflated to " + total + " of " + hdr.BlockSz + " bytes");
+            total += n;
+          }
         }
       }
     }
